[Music]  rather grew up in molenbeek and feels at  home here his songs are about the people  who live here the 18 year old loves  molenbeek like most of the migrants here  his family was originally from Morocco  we all know each other not personally  necessarily but by sight we say hello to  each other no one would reject anyone  for me Mullin Beck is like a family most  Europeans see molenbeek differently that  is something raita finds hard to bear  Saleh and Brahim Abdeslam also grew up  in molenbeek they were involved in the  terrorist attacks in Paris in 2015 and  in Belgium in 2016 over 160 people were  killed and hundreds injured but Rader  says that doesn't mean other residents  sympathize with Islamist terror they  came from Mullin Beck unfortunately they  could have come from anywhere the  outside world thinks were all terrorists  but the Optus long brothers are not the  only radicals from Mullen Beck over 30  young jihadists went to Syria to fight  for the so-called Islamic state more  than from most other places in Europe  Caius boumaaza is a taxi driver he's  lived in molenbeek for almost 20 years  he says there is a very real danger of  radicalization he took us to a former  car workshop that's now used as a prayer  room DWS camera team was not welcome  cai-you says that young people here are  indoctrinated and there is rampant hate  speech against Western democracies he  says that Europe has to defend itself  this is Europe's weakness Europe is very  weak it can't impose its values people  are scared and others are making the  most of this to radicalize youths and to  commit crimes  politically case has hung the Belgian  flag on his balcony his answer to the  terrorist attacks which shattered his  world like many here he knew the  Abdeslam brothers personally he says  that he has tried to warn the  authorities and help the police since  the attacks but he says nobody will  listen to him he thinks that politicians  are worried of being accused of  Islamophobia if they clamp down on  prayer rooms where radical ideology is  preached they don't say that there's a  real terrorism problem in Belgium and in  Europe they're scared of not satisfying  the people of being unpopular boumaaza  is not alone in worrying about the  younger generation the social worker  Bashir in rabbit at this youth centre is  also doing his best to prevent  radicalization  he shows Raida and his friends a film  which is supposed to help young people  recognize propaganda on the Internet and  avoid falling into the trap of hate  preachers miss do you have a distance to  this or could this sort of stuff  influence you never the situation has  become calmer in Muhlenberg since the is  lost ground in Syria Olivier von der  Hagen says that has lost its attraction  for many youths here in Belgium he's in  charge of promoting equality in the  community he says one reason some young  Muslims are radicalized is because they  are not treated equally on paper Belgian  society is extremely tolerant there's  freedom of speech freedom of religion  but in actual fact there is exclusion  discrimination and racism vada and his  friends wrote this song which is about  how difficult it is to find a place in  society  I feel Moroccan oh really but you were  born here but I feel Moroccan  he's just so they feel half-and-half  they're very Belgian on the national  team plays they're really proud of the  Belgian Red Devils but there's still a  lot to be done in terms of identity  building nothing's clear yet they're  often not counted as Belgians this  doesn't help much the European  Commission is just a few kilometers away  and yet it's another world for the use  of molenbeek one they know very little  about and one that barely interest them  Rayder doesn't know what an ideal Europe  could mean but he wants a better future  for himself and for immigrants and  Muslims in general we should all be  equal and everybody's opinion should  matter and we should all take decisions  together  who knows maybe he'll write a song about  Europe one day and about how all people  live together on this continent  [Music]

